abstract: >
  A governance model for how AI capabilities get built at an institution, as
  distinct from how AI gets used. Structured as a model framework intended to
  be adopted with local amendments, the way a jurisdiction adopts a model
  building code. It borrows working mechanisms from construction governance:
  a statement of need before design, risk classification by objective
  trigger, design review against the stated need, authorizations that carry
  conditions and expire, verification points before behavior is concealed,
  production approval as an act distinct from completion, a record of what
  was actually deployed, and certification of reusable components so that
  consumers inherit a certification rather than re-entering review. It also
  covers runtime dependencies on components the institution does not
  operate, where inspection has to move to the boundary.

  Its central design commitment is that gates belong at points of
  irreversibility rather than at milestones, following published research
  finding that external heavyweight change approval correlates with worse
  delivery performance without reducing change failure rates. Human review
  is therefore reserved for questions no automated check can answer, and
  the model declines to document any control its target platforms do not
  actually enforce.
